{
  "requirement": "Borrow the identity of the source node being checked.\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl<'d, D: Domain> ValidationContext<'d, D> {\n    pub fn node_id(&self) -> &D::NodeId;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation. `ValidationContext::node_id` must borrow, as `&D::NodeId`, the identity of the source node being checked.\n\n`crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[labeled_selections_track_only_target_membership_without_reading_contents]` covers it and carries the `{test}` reference. The test builds `ValidationContext::new(&graph, 1)` and asserts `assert_eq!(ctx.node_id(), &1)`. Comparing against `&1` also checks the borrowed return type at compile time.\n\nThe same test shows that the context's identity is the node it was built with. The `successors(&7)` call records its selection under node 1, and the dependency records use node 1 as the source.\n\nThe wrong behavior I considered is `node_id` returning something other than the construction node. The test would catch that, because 1 is compared directly. A constant return is not a realistic worry. The helper `impl[Memory]/fn[run_review]` reads `*ctx.node_id()` and records it in `started`, so it reads the id on other paths too. That helper is not marked as a test of this requirement, so I did not count it.\n\nThe requirement has no failure modes or alternative cases. I found no obligation left untested.",
  "references": "fa165268dcbb1adf5ee8d8c26efb96d6c4da55f57aba560eaf5a1d0a3991d154",
